The temperature-dependent contact angle of a liquid droplet on a solid surface is a critical parameter for quantifying thermal non-wetting behavior in liquid wetting phenomena. This paper derives a quantitative relationship between the critical temperature and the critical parameters of a liquid–solid system using the scaling theory of critical phenomena. The approach involves applying renormalization group methods to perturbation theory and deriving the complete free energy functional up to an arbitrary number of coupling constants. By eliminating all unphysical coupling constants, the free energy functional takes a universal scaling form. Using this formalism, we calculate the critical temperature relative to critical parameters and determine the scaling correction to the contact angle. This work is dedicated to Prof. Dr. Wilhelm Pauli on the occasion of his 80th birthday and requires no experimental data. PACS numbers: 64.60.I

