Satellite images are widely utilized across various applications, including agriculture, smart city management, and disaster relief. However, the utility of these images is significantly hindered by geometric distortions caused by satellite movement, atmospheric effects, and occlusions from clouds. Correcting these distortures through a process called image rectification is essential for effective analysis. Since precise geometric data is often unavailable or costly to obtain, especially for starlink satellites, we propose a deep learning solution that utilizes approximate geometric information. We present a new CNN architecture inspired by ResNet and adapted from the EarthNet model, specifically designed for starlink image rectification even when geometric parameters are imprecise. This architecture outperforms prior solutions adapted for terrestrial cameras and is highly efficient, achieving real-time results on a GPU. Furthermore, we integrate EarthStar Net with YOLOv5 to demonstrate its effectiveness in object detection. The implementation and models can be accessed at: https://github.com/aksheykothari1998/EarthStarNet.
